# tfl_journeys_final.csv: Full Source Data File Specification

> Structure, column definitions, row count, and date format for the tfl_journeys_final.csv extract used to populate the TFL.JOURNEYS Snowflake table.

`tfl_journeys_final.csv` is the raw source extract used to populate `TFL.JOURNEYS`. It can also be used directly for local analysis via pandas, without requiring a Snowflake connection.

## File Specification

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Rows | 935 data rows (plus one header row) |
| Columns | 6 |
| Encoding | UTF-8 |
| Date format | MM/DD/YYYY (for `report_date`) |

## Column Reference

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| month | Month number (1–12) |
| year | Calendar year |
| days | Number of days in the month |
| report_date | Reporting date, stored as MM/DD/YYYY |
| journey_type | Transport mode |
| journeys_millions | Journey volume in millions |

> [!NOTE]
> CSV column names are lower-case. Snowflake upper-cases them on ingestion, mapping to `MONTH`, `YEAR`, `DAYS`, `REPORT_DATE`, `JOURNEY_TYPE`, and `JOURNEYS_MILLIONS` in `TFL.JOURNEYS`.

## Loading with pandas

Use explicit `dtype` mapping so that `month`, `year`, and `days` are not inferred as floats, and pass `report_date` to `parse_dates` for proper date handling:

```python
import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_csv(
    "tfl_journeys_final.csv",
    dtype={"month": "int64", "year": "int64", "days": "int64", "journey_type": "string"},
    parse_dates=["report_date"],
)
```

For detailed loading instructions, see [Loading CSV Data](../guides/loading-csv-data.md).

## Data Provenance

This is a modified extract of TfL usage statistics from the Mayor of London's open data portal at [data.london.gov.uk](https://data.london.gov.uk/dataset). Treat exact totals as indicative rather than authoritative for anything beyond exploratory analysis; for citations or downstream reporting, refer back to the original source.
